{"name":"Blue Beads","hex":"#5a809e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"beads","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of bead"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A beaded necklace."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beads"}],"entries":[{"title":"Clintonia borealis","blocks":[{"text":"Clintonia borealis is a species of flowering plant in the lily family. The specific epithet borealis means \"of the north,\" which alludes to the fact that the species tends to thrive in the boreal forests of eastern Canada and northeastern United States. It is commonly known as bluebead, blue bead-lily, or yellow clintonia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Clintonia_borealis_140615.jpg/330px-Clintonia_borealis_140615.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clintonia_borealis"},{"title":"Bead","blocks":[{"text":"A bead is a small, decorative object that is formed in a variety of shapes and sizes of a material such as stone, bone, shell, glass, plastic, wood, or pearl and with a small hole for threading or stringing. Beads range in size from under 1 mm to over 1 cm in diameter."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/Beads.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bead"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Clintonia_borealis_%284639708749%29.jpg/330px-Clintonia_borealis_%284639708749%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Clintonia_borealis_%284639708749%29.jpg/960px-Clintonia_borealis_%284639708749%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Clintonia_borealis_%284639708749%29.jpg/330px-Clintonia_borealis_%284639708749%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AClintonia_borealis_(4639708749).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Clintonia_borealis_-_Blue_bead_lily_%284870007793%29.jpg/250px-Clintonia_borealis_-_Blue_bead_lily_%284870007793%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Clintonia_borealis_-_Blue_bead_lily_%284870007793%29.jpg/500px-Clintonia_borealis_-_Blue_bead_lily_%284870007793%29.jpg?utm_source=en.w